{
 "name": "edge_bevel_nonplanar_divergence",
 "description": "Three measured cells of edge bevel on NON-PLANAR bases, and the wider sweep's suspected shared root -- which plane the offset corner is placed in when the incident faces are not flat -- has since been MEASURED and PORTED, which split the three apart rather than confirming them as one law. (a) WIDTH mode at 0.08 on a folded quad+triangle base: the corners still part by 0.079188, essentially the whole width, so the two engines convert a perpendicular width into a corner slide against different dihedrals. (b) Round level 2 at 0.08 on a folded pair: the arc points still part by 0.016540, 21% of the bevel. (c) The whole STAR of an interior vertex (4 edges, so every incident face has BOTH of its ring edges bevelled) at 0.06 on a saddle: CLOSED by task 1170, gap now EMPTY in both the vertex and the face channel, where it was 0.007414. That (c) closed while (a) and (b) did not move by a single coordinate is the content of the split: (c) is the only one of the three with a mitred corner, so it is the only one the offset law reaches. (a) and (b) each pose their own unmeasured question -- how a perpendicular width becomes a slide, and where an arc's interior points go -- and neither is answered here. Counts agree in all three (7/9/3, 14/19/6 and 25/40/16); it is only positions that move, and the face rings that follow them.",
 "source": "Reference side: headless command-port capture of the edge-bevel tool (dirty-geometry sweep, task 1123, cases w2_ebev_width_triquad_np, ebev_round2_np_pair_e1, w3_ebev_star_np_saddle). vibe3d side: live drive of this build.",
 "provenance": {
  "schema": 1,
  "source": "live-capture",
